WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:07.819
Hollywood loves sequels, even if the audience
doesn't always agree. Here's a handful of

00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:12.450
would-be franchise expanders that didn't come
anywhere near duplicating the success of their

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:13.030
predecessors.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:15.730
A Christmas Story 2: My Summer Story

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:19.340
Being a Hollywood producer has its risks,
but at least you won't shoot your eye out

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.359
if you make a sadly subpar and utterly unnecessary
sequel to a beloved holiday classic.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.480
"Oh my God I shot my eye out!"

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:27.200
"You'll shoot your eye out kid."

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.140
"You'll shoot your eye out! You'll shoot your
eye out!"

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:35.720
Our proof lies in My Summer Story, the long-delayed
follow-up to A Christmas Story. Originally

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.730
titled It Runs in the Family, this 1994 release
did have a couple ties to the original, like

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:44.370
being based on stories by Jean Shepherd, who
returned to provide narration.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:48.390
But given that the entire cast of the original
was too old to reprise their roles, a whole

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:53.460
new set of stars had to be moved in, including
Charles Grodin and Mary Steenburgen. Reviews

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:58.730
weren't entirely unkind, but the film's box
office gross, which totaled less than $71,000,

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.530
speaks for itself.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:02.010
Legally Blondes

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.490
After 2003's Legally Blonde 2: Red, White
&amp; Blonde, Reese Witherspoon was just about

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:10.850
done with the character of bimbo-turned-lobbyist
Elle Woods. But after watching the franchise

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:14.750
continue without her with the successful Legally
Blonde stage musical, she took the reins once

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.390
more as producer of this 2009 direct-to-DVD
spinoff.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:22.799
The most interesting—and sad—thing about
the movie is the involvement of director "Savage"

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:27.189
Steve Holland, whose once-proud filmography
kicked off with the John Cusack classic Better

00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:32.799
Off Dead. Here, viewers watch as Elle's twin
cousins move into her old house and…

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:33.899
(sigh)

00:01:33.899 --> 00:01:38.249
You know what? It really isn't worth talking
about the plot. Suffice it to say that small

00:01:38.249 --> 00:01:41.490
dogs and a life lesson in the final act are
involved.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:43.249
Ace Ventura, Jr.

00:01:43.249 --> 00:01:47.340
More than a decade after Jim Carrey exited
the Ace Ventura franchise, someone got the

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.149
bright idea to continue the series without
him by attempting to tell the story of his

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:55.789
lookalike kid, who gets mixed up in solving
some pet-related wrongdoings after Ace Sr.

00:01:55.789 --> 00:02:00.709
flies the coop. It's a horrible idea for a
sequel, even one that went direct to video,

00:02:00.709 --> 00:02:03.959
and it was greeted with all the commercial
indifference and critical scorn you might

00:02:03.959 --> 00:02:07.299
expect. If it had arrived in the '90s, you
probably would have seen it at your favorite

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:11.720
Blockbuster. Alas, by the late aughts, it
could only gather dust on the loneliest DVD

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.060
rack at your local Best Buy.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.350
War Games: The Dead Code

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:20.580
Unlike a lot of technologically-driven films,
War Games remains a pretty solid little action

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:25.050
thriller, not least because it boasts the
combined talents of Matthew Broderick, Ally

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:29.240
Sheedy, Dabney Coleman, and Barry Corbin.
None of the above returned for the wholly

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:33.650
lamentable War Games: The Dead Code, which
tried to duplicate the teen hacker excitement

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:38.020
of the original by essentially copying the
plot…right down to the reintroduction of

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:42.020
War Games senior computer wiz Stephen Falken,
played here by a new actor who didn't even

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:43.820
appear in the original.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:47.290
Needless to say, these new War Games had no
winners.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:49.280
Addams Family Reunion

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:54.460
Seeking to reboot the Addams Family franchise
after 1991's The Addams Family and 1993's

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:58.740
The Addams Family Values, Fox decided to start
it over again as a new TV show called The

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.610
New Addams Family, featuring a new cast than
who had appeared in the movies.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:06.630
And then the studio filmed the made-for-TV
movie Addams Family Reunion, which was supposed

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:11.250
to serve as the series pilot...even though
it had yet another cast of actors in the lead

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:16.520
roles. Confused yet? Just be glad you aren't
Tim Curry or Darryl Hannah, both of whom somehow

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:20.950
ended up roped into this alternately unfunny
and offensive alleged "comedy."

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:25.030
Unsurprisingly, neither the movie nor the
show it launched had much of a legacy. The

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:29.270
New Addams Family exited the airwaves after
a two-season run, and there have been no family

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:31.420
reunions since.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.430
Road House 2: Last Call

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:36.760
There are definitely people in the world that
would argue that Patrick Swayze's Road House

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:40.400
is a classic. It's hard to imagine, though,
that many of them would go so far as to say

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.140
we needed a sequel, let alone one starring
Johnathon Schaech. If you remember him at

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:49.650
all, it's probably from That Thing You Do—you
know, the whiny guitar player who dated Liv

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.440
Tyler. Well...now you can add Road House 2
to your list. Or don't. Probably don't.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:58.310
Schaech stars as Swayze's character's son,
a DEA agent who ends up running his uncle's

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:02.430
club after a local drug kingpin beats him
half to death. If you're guessing that the

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:06.070
kid ultimately discovers that the kingpin
is the same bad guy who murdered his dad,

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:10.250
then you're a step ahead of the target audience
for Road House 2: Last Call. Maybe go a step

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.330
further and skip this one, and just watch
That Thing You Do again.

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:18.530
(music playing)

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:20.620
"Oh come on that's too fast"

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:21.819
Tooth Fairy 2

00:04:21.819 --> 00:04:26.020
You're setting a pretty low bar for comedy
when you hire Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson to

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.999
run around in fairy wings. But even when it's
held up against the relaxed standards set

00:04:29.999 --> 00:04:35.259
by the original Tooth Fairy, its little-seen
sequel is an ordeal. The Rock obviously wasn't

00:04:35.259 --> 00:04:39.159
coming back for another round, so the producers
hired Larry the Cable Guy on the assumption

00:04:39.159 --> 00:04:44.060
that, like the Rock, he'd provoke laughs simply
by strapping on fairy wings. It didn't work.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:46.479
And neither did hiring a pig as one of Larry's
co-stars.

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:48.060
Splash, Too

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:52.650
Splash, Too was Disney's attempt to cobble
together a sequel to its hit mermaid rom-com

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:57.219
starring Tom Hanks and Darryl Hannah. With
neither star affordable enough for a TV movie,

00:04:57.219 --> 00:05:01.969
the studio almost completely overhauled the
cast. Originally broadcast as a two-part event,

00:05:01.969 --> 00:05:06.229
Splash, Too has been all but forgotten. Even
the reissue fanatics at Disney haven't gotten

00:05:06.229 --> 00:05:08.800
around to putting it out on DVD or Blu-ray.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.409
National Lampoon's Christmas Vacation 2: Cousin
Eddie's Island Adventure

00:05:12.409 --> 00:05:14.819
(that's a mouthful)

00:05:14.819 --> 00:05:18.529
Cousin Eddie, the braying hick who drove Clark
crazy in a pair of National Lampoon's Vacation

00:05:18.529 --> 00:05:23.379
movies, could be fairly funny in limited doses.
Naturally, this was all the justification

00:05:23.379 --> 00:05:27.669
the studio needed for basing an entire Vacation
movie around Eddie after Chevy Chase and Beverly

00:05:27.669 --> 00:05:32.199
D'Angelo were finished with the franchise.
The horribly titled National Lampoon's Christmas

00:05:32.199 --> 00:05:33.990
Vacation 2: Cousin Eddie's Island Adventure

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.020
(panting)

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:40.710
starts with Eddie losing his job to a monkey
and goes downhill from there, with "star"

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:45.590
Randy Quaid huffing and puffing his way through
a series of increasingly lame pratfalls. Maybe

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:49.539
the movie would've turned out better if they
just replaced Quaid with a monkey altogether.

00:05:49.539 --> 00:05:51.849
Just kidding...we still love you Randy.

00:05:51.849 --> 00:05:54.319
And last, and equally least...

00:05:54.319 --> 00:05:56.539
Bachelor Party 2: The Last Temptation

00:05:56.539 --> 00:06:01.300
Studios cranked out a ton of sex comedies
in the '80s, and 1984's Bachelor Party would

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:04.849
have been just another one of them if its
star, Tom Hanks, hadn't gone on to superstardom

00:06:04.849 --> 00:06:09.569
later in the decade. More than 20 years later,
Fox tried making lightning strike twice with

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:13.800
Bachelor Party 2: The Last Temptation. It
was a sequel in name only that gathered up

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:17.199
a group of stock character types to mug for
the cameras in between gratuitous topless

00:06:17.199 --> 00:06:21.719
shots. Sadly for the studio, the next Hanks
doesn't seem to have been in the cast, but

00:06:21.719 --> 00:06:25.039
that doesn't mean we won't hear about a Bachelor
Party 3 in 2032...

00:06:25.039 --> 00:06:28.659
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:06:28.659 --> 00:06:31.529
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know which obscure movie sequels should've

00:06:31.529 --> 00:06:32.059
made the list...

